// One drill-down per app decided after Basalt's first release (Apps/...). The hierarchy app's existing
// views (hierarchy.c4, domain.c4, states, workflows and scenarios on folders) sit under Apps/Hierarchy.
views {

  view app-goals {
    title 'Apps/Goals/Overview'
    description '''
      What is going on while I'm away? The Goals app holds four kinds of record:
      - **Goal**: where a person is heading. One overarching goal per person; smaller goals nest under it.
      - **Branch** (alias mission): a pointer to work happening elsewhere (an agent session, a PR, a workflow
        run, a task done by hand). It opens at the idea and lives: idea (Considering) → mission → back →
        merged or dropped. Branches nest, and every branch serves a goal.
      - **Report**: a branch's latest state, a one-line summary, the time and a link. A branch that hasn't
        reported recently shows as stale; nothing shows working without evidence.
      - **Base**: the status quo as an explicit record. Nobody edits it; it changes only when a branch merges.
        Captured workflows fill it.

      Anything that speaks MCP reports through one `report` tool: local Claude Code and Codex through a hook,
      workflows from a step, connectors through webhooks. A "needs you" report lands in the pipeline lanes
      (Blocker, Ready, Considering).
    '''

    include laptopCli with { title 'Local Claude Code · Codex' }
    include signalbox.gateway.toolGateway with { title 'Tools gateway'; description '/mcp'; navigateTo layer-apps }
    include signalbox.ingress with { title 'Webhooks' }
    include signalbox.routine with { navigateTo app-routine }
    include signalbox.goals, signalbox.goals.*
    include hier.lanesA with { title 'Pipeline lanes' }

    exclude signalbox, signalbox.gateway, hier
    exclude * -> *
    include laptopCli -> signalbox.gateway.toolGateway with { title 'hook' }
    include signalbox.gateway.toolGateway -> signalbox.goals.report
    include signalbox.ingress -> signalbox.goals.report
    include signalbox.routine -> signalbox.goals.report, signalbox.routine -> signalbox.goals.base
    include signalbox.goals.* -> signalbox.goals.*
    include signalbox.goals.report -> hier.lanesA

    style laptopCli { color green }
    style signalbox.gateway.toolGateway, signalbox.ingress { color indigo }
    style hier.lanesA { color secondary }
    rank source { laptopCli }
    autoLayout TopBottom
  }

  view app-routine {
    title 'Apps/Personal routine/Overview'
    description '''
      How does a process become a workflow? A workflow is a graph of steps, and a person, an agent, code or a
      device does each step. It starts as a record of how things are done today, every step manual, and steps
      get automated one at a time. The single source is code in an app; the graph is drawn from that code, and
      every run is recorded. Rules that hold across steps are first-class, not notes.
      The first case is a personal routine: its runs show in Signalbox while the existing app keeps doing the
      work, and its devices become connectors later. Its runs report into Goals and its change merges into the base.
    '''

    include personA with { title 'Person' }
    include signalbox.routine
    include signalbox.goals with { navigateTo app-goals }
    include signalbox.goals.report, signalbox.goals.base

    exclude signalbox
    exclude * -> *
    include personA -> signalbox.routine
    include signalbox.routine -> signalbox.goals.report, signalbox.routine -> signalbox.goals.base

    style personA { color green }
    rank source { personA }
    global style depth
    autoLayout TopBottom
  }

  view app-hierarchy {
    title 'Apps/Hierarchy/Rules'
    description '''
      What does the hierarchy app add? It arranges records and grants into folders, one opinionated arrangement
      of the primitives; other arrangements stay possible. Its rules, not rules of the primitives:
      - Reach goes down the tree: a placement reaches its level and everything below.
      - The nearest placement wins, and resolution never depends on who sends.
      - Anything not above you comes in only by a loan; a mount is the only edge across owners.

      An app can use an account without it being placed anywhere. The app comes after caller identity and
      app-to-app calls, so it can enforce its grants inside other apps. Click a box for the next cut.
    '''

    include signalbox.tree with { title 'Hierarchy'; navigateTo m-tree }
    include domain.level with { navigateTo domain }
    include domain.placement with { navigateTo domain-placed }
    include domain.loan with { navigateTo hierarchy-resolution }
    include domain.mount with { navigateTo hierarchy }
    include domain.owner

    exclude domain

    style signalbox.tree { color primary }
    autoLayout TopBottom
  }
}
